--- abstract

The Discovery of Agents With Names (DAWN) working group focuses on how a
client can discover public properties of AI resources to facilitate subsequent
interactions such as selection, invocation, or communication.

A lot of discussion within the DAWN working group has been around how 
other phases of information exchange and lookup sit around the specific
discovery steps that are in scope for DAWN. 

This document provides a brief overview of the context of AI resource
discovery. It is intended as an anchor for further discussion. It is not
intended for adoption by any working group or later publication as an RFC.

This document makes no assumptions about implementation solutions and limits itself
to an abstract functional model.

--- middle

{: #sec-intro}
# Introduction

In order to understand the scope and purpose of the DAWN working group effort, 
it will be helpful to understand what we are calling "the broader discovery ecosystem."

Information must be registered to the discovery system, may be managed and updated,
can then be discovered by other AI agents. But the discoverable information must be
balanced as a compromise between too much detail which will overload the discovery 
process or be duplicative and thus cause maintenance issues, and too little
information which would make discovery suboptimal.

Initial searches (possibly in human language and intent-based) may rely on large
unstructured datasets. Additional detailed information may be retrieved or exchanged
following initial discovery, and this part of the process may also include negotiation
of capabilities.

In the end, this means that the "discovery ecosystem" can be broken down into a series of 
functions as set out in {{sec-lifecycle}}. These functions may overlap and, in some circumstances,
the information present in one function might obviate the need for another function.

From an implementation perspective, some of these functional components might be included 
within the same module, and it is possible that some elements might be achieved using the same
protocols. This document makes no assumptions about implementation solutions and limits itself
to an abstract functional model: frustrated engineers are invited to progress direct to solution
architectures and protocol specifications.

## Chicken and Egg

There are three approaches to cover the material in this document:

- Present and explain the functional step in the discovery ecosystem lifecycle
- Show the functional components and flow of control in a figure
- Explain the information elements that need to be stored or exchanged

This document includes all three approaches, but each approach depends on the other two, 
so it will be a matter of personal taste which the reader wants to see first.

